/* v4 learner-facing rewrites: Chronology (7/7 including c.two in base file) */
Object.assign(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY||(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY={}),{
'c.eras':{title:'Ten eras that make biblical history easier to hold in your head',dek:'A teaching timeline is not a perfect chronology. It is a set of large historical rooms that help you place stories, prophets, and books.',body:['Beginners often experience biblical history as a pile of names and dates. A simpler starting point is to divide the story into broad eras: primeval stories, patriarchs, exodus/wilderness, conquest/judges, united monarchy, divided kingdoms, exile, return/Persian period, life of Jesus, and the early Church.','These eras are teaching categories, not hard borders. Abraham’s dating is approximate. The exodus chronology is debated. Prophetic ministries overlap political periods. The point is orientation: if you know that Amos belongs in the divided-kingdom era and Haggai belongs after exile, you have gained useful context without pretending every date is settled.','As your knowledge grows, you can add precision inside the eras. The framework should become a scaffold for better evidence, not a substitute for it.'],plain:'Learn the big rooms of the timeline first. Add exact dates only where the evidence is strong enough to deserve them.',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'A ten-era teaching timeline',points:[{date:'Origins',title:'Primeval stories'},{date:'c. 2000–1700 BC',title:'Patriarchs'},{date:'2nd millennium BC',title:'Exodus & wilderness'},{date:'before monarchy',title:'Conquest & Judges'},{date:'c. 1050–930 BC',title:'United monarchy'},{date:'930–586 BC',title:'Divided kingdoms'},{date:'6th c. BC',title:'Exile'},{date:'538–400s BC',title:'Return / Persia'},{date:'1st c. AD',title:'Jesus'},{date:'AD 30–100',title:'Early Church'}]},challenge:{kind:'timeline-sort',title:'Put the eras in broad order',prompt:'Arrange the teaching eras from earliest to latest.',items:['Patriarchs','Exodus and wilderness','United monarchy','Divided kingdoms','Exile','Return/Persian period','Life of Jesus','Early Church'],answer:[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7],hints:['The monarchy comes before exile; return comes after exile.'],why:'Broad eras provide orientation even where exact dates remain debated.'}},
'c.anchor':{title:'A handful of dates can orient centuries of biblical history',dek:'Anchor dates are useful because other events can be placed before, after, or around them. They should be used more carefully than memorized numbers usually are.',body:['A few comparatively firm historical dates do enormous work: Assyria’s fall of Samaria in 722 BC; Babylon’s destruction of Jerusalem in 586 BC; Cyrus’s Persian decree/return era around 538 BC; the Second Temple completed around 516 BC; Nehemiah’s wall-building mission in the mid-fifth century BC; Rome’s destruction of the Second Temple in AD 70.','Not every biblical event has that kind of dating confidence. Patriarchal chronology is approximate, the exodus date is debated, and the exact year of Jesus’ birth is uncertain. Good timelines show those differences instead of giving every label the same visual certainty.','The purpose of an anchor is relational memory. If Isaiah is active before and around Assyria’s rise, if Jeremiah spans the approach to 586 BC, and if Haggai belongs after the return, you can place the prophets meaningfully even without memorizing every reign.'],plain:'Use a few firm dates as hooks. Do not make uncertain dates look equally certain just because they fit neatly on a line.',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'High-value anchors',points:[{date:'722 BC',title:'Samaria falls',note:'Assyria ends northern kingdom'},{date:'586 BC',title:'Jerusalem destroyed',note:'Babylon; temple burned'},{date:'538 BC',title:'Persian return era',note:'Cyrus'},{date:'516 BC',title:'Second Temple completed'},{date:'445 BC',title:'Nehemiah era',note:'Walls / Persian period'},{date:'AD 70',title:'Second Temple destroyed',note:'Rome'}]},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Match the anchor to the event',prompt:'Connect each date to its event.',items:['722 BC','586 BC','538 BC','AD 70'],options:['Assyria defeats northern Israel','Babylon destroys Jerusalem','Persian return era','Rome destroys the Second Temple'],answer:[0,1,2,3],hints:['586 BC is the Jerusalem/Babylon hinge.'],why:'Anchor dates become useful when attached to historical turning points, not memorized alone.'}},
'c.oop':{title:'Some books tell earlier stories from a later shelf position',dek:'The Bible is full of “out-of-order” relationships because shelf organization follows genre and tradition as well as historical sequence.',body:['Ruth appears after Judges and is set in the period of the judges, so shelf and story align fairly closely there. Chronicles comes after Kings but retells overlapping centuries. Job sits after Esther in the shelf even though its narrative setting is not a Persian-period sequel. Prophetic books are grouped together after wisdom books even though many prophets lived during events already narrated in Kings.','In the New Testament, the letters follow Acts even though some letters were written during the period Acts narrates. The Gospels appear before the letters even though several letters may be earlier compositions than the final written form of some Gospels.','These “out-of-order” examples are not mistakes. They demonstrate why four questions must stay separate: shelf position, narrated setting, historical event sequence, and composition date.'],plain:'The shelf is organized like a library, not a single chronological feed.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Shelf order vs. historical relationship',columns:[{title:'Where the shelf puts it',items:['Chronicles after Kings','Prophets after Wisdom','Letters after Acts']},{title:'What history may look like',items:['Chronicles overlaps Kings','Many prophets speak during Kings','Letters can be written during Acts-era events']}],shared:['Shelf order remains useful navigation']},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Spot the out-of-order assumption',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['Chronicles retells material that overlaps Samuel/Kings.','Many prophets lived during the monarchy.','A book printed later must describe later events.','A book printed earlier must have been written earlier.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Printed order does not prove historical or composition order.'],why:'Canonical arrangement and chronology are related but distinct systems.'}},
'c.proph':{title:'Place prophets on the historical backbone before reading their predictions',dek:'A prophet’s audience and political crisis often explain more than a modern prophecy chart does.',body:['Prophets appear in one shelf block, but their ministries span very different moments. Amos and Hosea speak before the northern kingdom falls. Isaiah and Micah address Judah in the Assyrian era. Jeremiah lives into Jerusalem’s Babylonian crisis. Ezekiel speaks among exiles. Haggai and Zechariah address rebuilding after return.','That placement changes interpretation. A warning about Assyria cannot be read as though Babylon were already the dominant empire. A post-exilic call to rebuild the temple cannot be treated as though the first temple were still standing. Historical setting constrains the range of responsible meanings.','Some prophetic books are difficult to date precisely. When scholarship differs, a timeline should show a range or label the uncertainty. Historical humility is better than fake precision.'],plain:'Before asking “When will this prophecy happen?”, ask “Who first heard it, and what crisis were they already living through?”',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'Prophetic voices around historical hinges',points:[{date:'8th c. BC',title:'Amos / Hosea',note:'Northern kingdom'},{date:'8th c. BC',title:'Isaiah / Micah',note:'Judah and Assyria'},{date:'late 7th–6th c. BC',title:'Jeremiah',note:'Babylon approaches'},{date:'exile',title:'Ezekiel',note:'Among deportees'},{date:'post-538 BC',title:'Haggai / Zechariah',note:'Rebuilding era'}]},challenge:{kind:'timeline-sort',title:'Place the prophets broadly',prompt:'Arrange these prophetic settings from earlier to later.',items:['Amos/Hosea before northern kingdom falls','Isaiah/Micah in Assyrian-era Judah','Jeremiah around Babylonian crisis','Ezekiel among exiles','Haggai/Zechariah after return'],answer:[0,1,2,3,4],hints:['Post-exilic prophets come last.'],why:'Historical placement gives prophetic rhetoric its first horizon.'}},
'c.paul':{title:'Paul’s letters belong to a moving first-century timeline',dek:'Pauline letters are not printed chronologically, so Acts, travel, communities, and letter dates have to be compared rather than assumed from shelf order.',body:['Paul’s letters appear Romans through Philemon on the shelf, but that sequence is not the sequence in which they were written. 1 Thessalonians is commonly considered among the earliest surviving Pauline letters, while Romans is later despite appearing first.','Acts supplies one narrative framework for Paul’s travels, conflicts, imprisonments, and communities. Matching letters to Acts is useful but not always exact; scholars debate the location/date of some letters and whether all letters traditionally associated with Paul were written directly by him.','A responsible Pauline timeline therefore uses ranges and degrees of confidence. The point is not to force every letter into one precise month but to know that Galatians, Corinthians, Romans, prison letters, and pastoral material address different phases and situations.'],plain:'Paul’s shelf order is not his writing calendar. Use historical reconstruction carefully and label disputed dates.',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'A simplified Pauline writing arc',points:[{date:'early mission era',title:'1 Thessalonians?',note:'Often dated early'},{date:'mid-50s',title:'Corinthian correspondence',note:'Community conflict and questions'},{date:'mid/late-50s',title:'Romans',note:'Written to Rome before arrival'},{date:'later / disputed placement',title:'Prison letters',note:'Location/date debated in places'},{date:'later / authorship debated',title:'Pastoral letters',note:'Different scholarly judgments'}]},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Calibrate Pauline chronology',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['Romans appears first in the Pauline shelf but was not necessarily written first.','Some Pauline letter dates are debated.','Shelf order gives an exact writing chronology.','Every letter can be matched to one Acts scene with certainty.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Canonical arrangement is not a timestamp.'],why:'Pauline chronology is a historical reconstruction with both strong anchors and disputed placements.'}},
'c.all':{title:'Build one timeline without pretending all dates are equally certain',dek:'Whole-Bible chronology is a layered model: ancient story eras, better-anchored monarchic/exilic events, the first-century Jesus movement, and composition histories that do not always match narrated time.',body:['A whole-Bible timeline should show more than one kind of information. It can place narrated events, major political anchors, prophetic ministries, and New Testament settings while separately indicating where composition is later or debated.','The earliest periods require broad ranges and humility. The monarchy and exile are better connected to external historical anchors. The first century has Roman rulers, inscriptions, Jewish history, and early Christian sources that permit another level of orientation. Yet even there, exact dates such as Jesus’ birth year or the date of every Gospel remain debated.','Good mastery is not reciting one “correct” timeline from memory. It is knowing which events are early/late relative to one another, which dates anchor the structure, and where uncertainty belongs.'],plain:'A trustworthy timeline shows both chronology and confidence. Precision should be earned by evidence.',visual:{type:'stack',title:'Layers of a responsible Bible timeline',layers:[{title:'Narrated era',text:'When the story says events occur'},{title:'Historical anchors',text:'Dates tied to external evidence and political history'},{title:'Prophetic / community setting',text:'Who is speaking into which crisis'},{title:'Composition',text:'When texts may have been written/shaped'},{title:'Confidence',text:'Firm anchor, approximate range, or disputed placement'}]},challenge:{kind:'capstone',title:'Choose the responsible timeline claim',prompt:'Which statement best represents mature chronology?',clues:['Some biblical dates are strong anchors.','Some eras are approximate or debated.','Shelf position and composition are different questions.'],options:['Every event should be assigned an exact year.','A useful timeline combines relative order, anchor dates, and visible uncertainty.','Shelf order is sufficient chronology.','Disputed dates should be hidden to avoid confusion.'],answer:1,hints:['Good teaching distinguishes confidence levels.'],why:'Chronological mastery includes knowing where precision is justified and where it is not.'}}
});
